FreeBSD-EN-26:27.tcp                                            Errata Notice
                                                          The FreeBSD Project

Topic:          TCP timers may overflow and fire late or not at all

Category:       core
Module:         tcp
Announced:      2026-10-14
Credits:        Lukas Book
Affects:        FreeBSD 15.1 and 14.4
Corrected:      2026-08-06 09:07:02 UTC (stable/15, 15.1-STABLE)
                2026-10-14 16:04:33 UTC (releng/15.1, 15.1-RELEASE-p5)
                2026-08-06 05:55:09 UTC (stable/14, 14.5-PRERELEASE)
                2026-10-14 16:03:51 UTC (releng/14.4, 14.4-RELEASE-p11)

For general information regarding FreeBSD Errata Notices and Security
Advisories, including descriptions of the fields above, security
branches, and the following sections, please visit
<URL:https://security.FreeBSD.org/>.

I.   Background

Each TCP connection maintains a set of timers, covering retransmission,
persist, keepalive, 2MSL (TIME_WAIT and FIN_WAIT_2), and delayed ACK.
Since FreeBSD 14.0, the default TCP stack multiplexes all of these timers
onto a single callout(9) per connection.  Whenever a timer is started or
stopped, the stack selects the earliest pending timer, together with the
allowed scheduling precision, and reschedules the callout accordingly.

A stopped timer is marked by setting its expiry time to SBT_MAX.

II.  Problem Description

When a timer was stopped, its previously computed precision was left in
place.  While selecting the next timer to schedule, the stack added that
precision to the SBT_MAX expiry of the stopped timer, causing a signed
integer overflow.  The resulting negative value was then used to compute
the precision passed to callout(9) for the connection's callout.

III. Impact

The per-connection callout may be scheduled with an invalid precision,
causing TCP timers to run far later than intended.  Affected connections
may fail to retransmit, send keepalives, or leave TIME_WAIT and FIN_WAIT_2
in a timely manner, and can remain in the connection table indefinitely,
consuming memory, protocol control blocks, and local ports.

Affected connections can be recognized by negative or implausibly large
values in the timer columns (rexmt, persist, keep, 2msl, delack) reported
by "netstat -nxptcp".

Only connections using the default FreeBSD TCP stack are affected.

IV.  Workaround

Affected connections can be listed with netstat(1) and terminated with
tcpdrop(8).  The following prints the local and foreign endpoints of all
connections with a negative value in any of the rexmt, persist, keep,
2msl, or delack timer columns:

# netstat -nxptcp | \
  awk 'NR > 2 && ($14 < 0 || $15 < 0 || $16 < 0 || $17 < 0 || $18 < 0) \
  { print $4, $5 }'

Review the output, then drop each connection by passing the two endpoints
exactly as printed to tcpdrop(8), which accepts the netstat(1) address
format directly, for example:

# tcpdrop 192.0.2.1.80 198.51.100.7.53211

Note that connections whose timers have been deferred without becoming
negative are not detected by this method, and that dropping a connection
resets it for the peer.

V.   Solution

Upgrade your system to a supported FreeBSD stable or release / security
branch (releng) dated after the correction date, and reboot the system.

Perform one of the following:

1) To update your system installed from base system packages:

Systems running a 15.0-RELEASE or later version of FreeBSD on the amd64 or
arm64 platforms, which were installed using base system packages, can be
updated via the pkg(8) utility:

# pkg upgrade -r FreeBSD-base
# shutdown -r +10min "Rebooting for an erratum fix"

2) To update your system installed from binary distribution sets:

Systems running a RELEASE version of FreeBSD on the amd64 or arm64 platforms
which were not installed using base system packages can be updated via the
freebsd-update(8) utility:

# freebsd-update fetch
# freebsd-update install
# shutdown -r +10min "Rebooting for an erratum fix"

3) To update your system via a source code patch:

The following patches have been verified to apply to the applicable
FreeBSD release branches.

a) Download the relevant patch from the location below, and verify the
detached PGP signature using your PGP utility.

# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/EN-26:27/tcp.patch
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/EN-26:27/tcp.patch.asc
# gpg --verify tcp.patch.asc

b) Apply the patch.  Execute the following commands as root:

# cd /usr/src
# patch -E -p0 < /path/to/patch

c) Recompile your kernel as described in
<URL:https://www.FreeBSD.org/handbook/kernelconfig.html> and reboot the
system.

VI.  Correction details

This issue is corrected as of the corresponding Git commit hash in the
following stable and release branches:

Branch/path                             Hash                     Revision
- -------------------------------------------------------------------------
stable/15/                              e8d1cee43741    stable/15-n284741
releng/15.1/                            cf95af02eb15  releng/15.1-n283632
stable/14/                              85485a570d4f    stable/14-n274777
releng/14.4/                            11f0c61eecb8  releng/14.4-n273783
- -------------------------------------------------------------------------

Run the following command to see which files were modified by a
particular commit:

# git show --stat <commit hash>

Or visit the following URL, replacing NNNNNN with the hash:

<URL:https://cgit.freebsd.org/src/commit/?id=NNNNNN>

To determine the commit count in a working tree (for comparison against
nNNNNNN in the table above), run:

# git rev-list --count --first-parent HEAD

